behavior. Let me verify requirement 4: median([]) raises ValueError — yes it does. Functions don't modify input — yes.\n\nActually, hold on. I need to reconsider whether the fix I wrote is actually correct or whether there's a subtle issue. Let me re-read: the written median is correct. Good.\n\nHowever, I'm suspicious: did I actually make the fix, or did the \"write\" happen before discovering the root cause? The plan status shows \"定位唯一根因并最小修复 stats.py\" as todo, and latest_test=0. The plan wasn't updated to done. So the write at id 21 may have been the fix, but I haven't confirmed.\n\nHmm, but actually the write result says written stats.py with the corrected content. Let me just run pytest and run_checks to verify, then update plan and finish.\n\nActually wait — the task states the baseline had many failures. Let me check: was there a baseline pytest run showing failures? latest_test=0 means last pytest rc=0. baseline: true. Hmm.\n\nGiven confusion, safest: run pytest -q, then run run_checks.py, then finish if both pass.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the write may have been done but maybe it wasn't actually persisted correctly. Let me just run the commands.\n\nLet me first run pytest to confirm current state.\n\nActually the format requires one JSON per round. Let me run run_checks.py.\n\nWait, but I should reconsider: is the written file genuinely the minimal fix? The requirement says \"只修改 stats.py\". The write replaced whole file, which is fine.\n\nLet me proceed: run run_checks.py.\n\nHmm, but actually maybe the issue is different. Let me reconsider the run_checks output: only median mismatches, and notably mean never mismatches. So root cause = median even-length. The written file fixed it. Good.\n\nBut let me double check: maybe the file was NOT actually fixed and read shows cached? No, read shows actual content.\n\nOK run run_checks.py.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the whole situation. The provided \"current execution state\" says writes:1, baseline: